ers they aren't scientific questions  the big bang theory itself does seem  like fantasy  not real scientific work  there were scientific advantages for a  big bang theory if it were true then it  would offer a solution to a problem  called ober's paradox named after the  german astronomer heinrich wilhelm  olbers  problem simple  proposition 1 space is big  really big  proposition two there are lots of stars  out there lots and lots of stars so  putting one and two together if you  point to any spot in the sky and travel  in that direction far enough you'll  eventually hit a star  proposition three  stars shine  that is they emit light right that's  what we see up in the sky now putting  one two and three together if every  point in the sky leads to a star and the  light of that star is visible in the  night sky then the entire night sky  ought to be glowing  it should be as bright in the night as  it is in the day  so why is night dark  big bang theory gives us a nice easy  answer  the universe is of a finite age it might  be true that there's a star along any  line you point out but it takes an  amount of time for the light to get to  earth and if the star is far enough away  the light hasn't had time to get here  the idea behind the big bang basically  goes like this  as the universe expands and space gets  bigger the matter in it gets diluted  this means that in the future the  density of the universe will be lower  than it is today  and that in the past it was higher than  it is now  this also means that the universe was  hotter in the past  if we go back far enough in time  we would eventually find a period in  which the entire universe was filled  with an ultra hot soup of matter and  radiation  all at temperatures of thousands of  degrees or higher  so hot that even atoms themselves  couldn't stay intact  these are the kinds of conditions that  today you would find inside of a star  this hot era in our universe's early  history is what cosmologists now call  the big bang  this big bang theory makes many testable  predictions including that of a  background of microwave radiation that  should have been generated as universe  expanded and cooled and today this  background fills all of space  this cosmic microwave background was  first detected in 1964  and has been measured a greater and  greater precision ever since  as this and other evidence accumulated  in support of the big bang more and more  scientists became convinced of the  validity of this theory today there's  essentially universal agreement among  scientists at our universe evolved from  a hot state  over a period of about 13.8 billion  years  the idea of a cosmic microwave  background can be traced back to ralph  alpher and robert herman  two american cosmologists who first  proposed the idea in 1948  working forward from the big bang and  using edwin hubble's famous equation and  constant for describing the expansion of  the universe  they estimated a black body temperature  of 5 kelvin for the background radiation  of the modern universe  it would be over a decade before several  scientists either rediscovered their  work or made their own predictions on  how this background radiation could be  observed with estimates moving ever  closer to the radiation's true  temperature of 2.7 kelvin  one of these scientists was a physicist  named robert dickey who had studied the  question of the temperature of space for  years and invented the most widely used  microwave detector design while working  at mit  by 1964 he was at princeton and hard at  work on a painstaking scientific mission  to search for and hopefully detect the  cosmic microwave background and with it  the first robust observational proof of  the big bang  several colleagues at princeton had  joined him in the search  jim peebles had recently written about  how to detect and identify this  radiation and was preparing to publish  his results  while david todd wilkinson and peter  roll began buildin